WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.434
-Our next guest
is an award-winning TV

00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:03.937
and comic-book writer.

00:00:03.937 --> 00:00:05.839
His graphic novel "Adora and
the Distance" is on sale now.

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:08.274
Please welcome to the show
Marc Bernardin.

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:09.942 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:09.942 --> 00:00:11.911
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:11.911 --> 00:00:17.750 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:23.623 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.958
Welcome to the show.
-Hey! This is awesome.

00:00:25.958 --> 00:00:28.961
-I'm so happy to have you here.
You -- Let's be honest.

00:00:28.961 --> 00:00:33.166
You launched a successful
social-media campaign

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:34.901
to get yourself on this show.
-I did.

00:00:34.901 --> 00:00:37.703
[ Laughter ]

00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:39.205
There's a comic-book writer
named Mark Waid

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:40.673
who always says
that breaking into comics

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.408
is like breaking out of prison.

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:43.809
No two people do it
the same way,

00:00:43.809 --> 00:00:45.378
and they seal up the hole
after you're done.

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.580
-Right.
-So there is no way that

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:49.815
this is gonna work again,
but I had to talk about

00:00:49.815 --> 00:00:51.618
this comic book
for, like, a month and a half.

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:53.052
I was like,
"Well, let's gamify it.

00:00:53.052 --> 00:00:54.286
Let's have a good time with it.

00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:55.688
Let's see if I can
get on 'Seth Meyers.'"

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:58.258
What are the odds that at
the beginning of this thing --

00:00:58.258 --> 00:00:59.591
Like, "Yeah, no,
Seth will see it,

00:00:59.591 --> 00:01:01.360
and Seth will be like,
'Yeah, man, come on the show.'

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.762
Let's talk about
your comic book.'"

00:01:02.762 --> 00:01:04.597
And now I'm on "Seth Meyers"
talking about my comic book.

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:08.168
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Now, it should be noted, I --

00:01:08.168 --> 00:01:10.136
One of the things
that helped this campaign

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:12.538
is I am a big comic-book fan.

00:01:12.538 --> 00:01:14.974
I follow a lot of comic-book
writers on Twitter.

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:16.675
I'm friends with a lot
of comic-book writers.

00:01:16.675 --> 00:01:19.145
And it kept coming across
my Twitter feed,

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.246
because a lot of people
I liked and trusted

00:01:21.246 --> 00:01:22.448
liked and trusted you.

00:01:22.448 --> 00:01:24.384
So that helped a great deal,
as well.

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:26.019
You seem very beloved
within the field.

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.420
-All of the checks
have cleared.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.289
-Yeah.
-So I feel good about that now.

00:01:29.289 --> 00:01:32.124
Not wasted money at all.
-Yeah.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:34.293
Now, so this -- You know,
this is, it should be noted,

00:01:34.293 --> 00:01:36.128
one of the reasons
you want to talk about it.

00:01:36.128 --> 00:01:37.697
This is a special comic.

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:40.833
This was inspired by
your daughter who has autism.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:42.201
-Indeed.

00:01:42.201 --> 00:01:43.970
-And why did you want
to tell a story about her

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.973
and her autism
in this comic format?

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:49.275
-Well, when she was diagnosed --
At this point, she's 19.

00:01:49.275 --> 00:01:50.275
So this was like 15 years ago.

00:01:50.275 --> 00:01:51.577
Like, lots of my friends
were like,

00:01:51.577 --> 00:01:52.778
"Well, you should
write about it.

00:01:52.778 --> 00:01:54.347
You're a journalist.
You know how to do this.

00:01:54.347 --> 00:01:56.415
You should write
some kind of memoir

00:01:56.415 --> 00:01:57.684
about raising
an autistic child."

00:01:57.684 --> 00:01:59.351
And I'm like, "I am
the least interesting person

00:01:59.351 --> 00:02:00.753
in this story."

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:02.188
I don't know what's happening
in her head.

00:02:02.188 --> 00:02:03.689
I never do.
She's not that verbal.

00:02:03.689 --> 00:02:06.258
She can't tell me
what she's feeling.

00:02:06.258 --> 00:02:08.027
She can't tell me if she is sad
or if she's happy.

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.096
All I know is "I want chicken
nuggets" and "I'm sleepy."

00:02:10.096 --> 00:02:11.697
-Mm-hmm.
-Like, that's what I got.

00:02:11.697 --> 00:02:14.734
And so I then imagined what
this interior life for her was,

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:18.738
and it became this sort of
process of building the metaphor

00:02:18.738 --> 00:02:20.306
of, here's a girl on a quest,

00:02:20.306 --> 00:02:22.308
trying to get to the end
of her journey.

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.243
And what's at the end
of this journey?

00:02:24.243 --> 00:02:26.613
And it's -- Because I'm a nerd
and I grew up on, like,

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:27.947
"Lord of the Rings"
and "Star Wars"

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.115
and "Raiders of the Lost Ark,"

00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:30.850
there's running and jumping
and adventure,

00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:32.352
and there's knights
and there's all of this stuff.

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:33.920
But it's ultimately about a girl

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.888
trying to get from one world
to the other.

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:38.024
-And your antagonist in this,

00:02:38.024 --> 00:02:40.559
which is, I think,
unique to a comic book,

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:43.862
when we talk about
the convention of it,

00:02:43.862 --> 00:02:46.131
isn't a character,
it's not a villain so much.

00:02:46.131 --> 00:02:49.201
It is this sort of
vaguer idea of the distance.

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:50.436
When did you come up with that?

00:02:50.436 --> 00:02:53.005
And explain, real quick,
what it represents.

00:02:53.005 --> 00:02:54.440
-I came up with it
very early on.

00:02:54.440 --> 00:02:57.310
The distance is, not to spoil
too much about it,

00:02:57.310 --> 00:03:00.313
but it's the place she is going.

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:02.315
In the story within the story,
it's a force.

00:03:02.315 --> 00:03:03.883
It's like a hurricane
or a tsunami.

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:06.986
It's a storm that's tearing
everything apart on its way

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:08.254
to get to this girl.

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:09.856
And so what she wants to do
to save her friends

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:12.825
and her family is confront
the distance where it is.

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:15.094
But I didn't want it to be bad.
I didn't want a bad guy.

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:17.229
I just wanted somebody
who had a dream

00:03:17.229 --> 00:03:19.999
and had a hope and wanted
to protect the people she loved.

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:22.434
I didn't need to slay dragons
to get that done.

00:03:22.434 --> 00:03:25.037
-So, I hope I'm saying
her name right. Ariela?

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:26.405
-Ariela Kristantina.

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:29.075
-So you have an incredible
illustrator here.

00:03:29.075 --> 00:03:31.177
And I want to ask, like,
when you, you know --

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:33.546
I've been lucky enough
one or two times to write stuff

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:35.448
that then gets, you know,
that an illustrator

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:37.249
turns in work,
which is so incredible.

00:03:37.249 --> 00:03:39.252
Were you very specific
with what you wanted,

00:03:39.252 --> 00:03:41.987
or did you just sort of tell her
to use her talents?

00:03:41.987 --> 00:03:43.690
-I was specific
about the things

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.224
that I knew were emotional.

00:03:45.224 --> 00:03:46.825
But then I said,
"Listen. You can draw.

00:03:46.825 --> 00:03:48.928
I can't.
So, please, make it awesome."

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:50.829
And she, like,
took it on her back

00:03:50.829 --> 00:03:52.898
and she, like, shouldered
this burden

00:03:52.898 --> 00:03:54.333
of doing all the research.

00:03:54.333 --> 00:03:58.904
Like, what does a 14th-century
port city look like? Okay?

00:03:58.904 --> 00:04:00.840
And she would, like, send me
these reference pages of,

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.274
here's the Moroccan
influence here,

00:04:02.274 --> 00:04:03.576
and there's a bit of Moors
in here

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:04.944
and here's a little bit of this.

00:04:04.944 --> 00:04:06.479
[ Gasps ]
Like, every page was like --

00:04:06.479 --> 00:04:08.548
[ Gasps ]
"Oh, my God. This is amazing!"

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:10.450
And, like, the main character,
Adora,

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:13.619
I had written her
sort of blankly.

00:04:13.619 --> 00:04:15.488
You know, it was,
she's 9 years old,

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:17.256
curly hair,
she goes on a quest.

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:19.858
And Ariela is the one who came
back with this little brown girl

00:04:19.858 --> 00:04:20.960
with this massive bush of hair.

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.428
And I was like,
"Where'd this come from?"

00:04:22.428 --> 00:04:24.096
She was like, "Well,
it's your daughter, right?

00:04:24.096 --> 00:04:25.698
So she should look like you."

00:04:25.698 --> 00:04:28.234
And then I started to cry,
because that's what happens.

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:29.969
And then just said,
"Okay, keep going.

00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:31.537
Like, don't even wait
for me to tell you things.

00:04:31.537 --> 00:04:33.138
Just make it awesome."
And she did.

00:04:33.138 --> 00:04:34.540
-That's great. You also --

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:37.643
You host a podcast with
friend of the show Kevin Smith.

00:04:37.643 --> 00:04:40.579
-Yes.
-And Kevin is a lot of fun

00:04:40.579 --> 00:04:42.381
when he's here.
I can only imagine --

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:44.216
You know, if anything,
when he's here,

00:04:44.216 --> 00:04:46.686
the only thing I'm sad about is
how little time I have with him.

00:04:46.686 --> 00:04:49.421
In a podcast form, you probably
get a full -- a full share.

00:04:49.421 --> 00:04:53.226
-Oh, I get two and a half hours
a clip of unadulterated Kevin.

00:04:53.226 --> 00:04:54.460
-Yeah.
-And I get to be sort of

00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:56.229
his weed GPS.
-Yeah.

00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:57.563
-You know, 'cause when he --
'cause, I don't know

00:04:57.563 --> 00:04:59.231
if you guys know this,
but Kevin likes to smoke.

00:04:59.231 --> 00:05:01.066
-Smoke weed?
-A little bit. A little bit.

00:05:01.066 --> 00:05:02.634
-Yeah.
This is breaking news here.

00:05:02.634 --> 00:05:05.237
-[ Laughs ]
You should be a journalist.

00:05:05.237 --> 00:05:08.440
And, so, like, I get to
pull him out of the fog bank

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.443
cul-de-sacs where it's like,
"Now, where were we?"

00:05:10.443 --> 00:05:11.711
-Right.

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:12.911
-It's like, "Oh, we were
talking about Moon Knight."

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:16.449
"Oh, right. That's cool.
I love Moon Knight."

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:18.283
He -- When I told him
I was coming, he was like,

00:05:18.283 --> 00:05:20.186
"you should bring Seth a joint,

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:23.622
because there's a joint on his
desk and it needs a friend."

00:05:23.622 --> 00:05:27.793
I was like, "Kevin, you are
an internationally famous actor,

00:05:27.793 --> 00:05:29.895
director, filmmaker, raconteur.

00:05:29.895 --> 00:05:34.266
I am a anonymous
husky Black gentleman

00:05:34.266 --> 00:05:37.169
who will be ferrying marijuana
across state lines.

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:40.173
I don't think so, pal.
Not gonna end so well for me."

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:41.807
-Yeah.
Well, your instincts are good,

00:05:41.807 --> 00:05:44.543
but I will say, I'm glad
Kevin checked on his joint.

00:05:44.543 --> 00:05:47.179
We still have it.
This is -- This --

00:05:47.179 --> 00:05:48.747
He brought this,
I think, three years ago.

00:05:48.747 --> 00:05:50.383
And --
-I'm sure it's great now.

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:53.252
-He dared me to keep it here
as long as I could,

00:05:53.252 --> 00:05:54.520
and I have it now.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:56.488
I feel like
it's just fully legal now.

00:05:56.488 --> 00:05:58.557
[ Laughter ]
-And now you can't smoke it,

00:05:58.557 --> 00:06:01.026
because you'll get superpowers.
-Exactly.

00:06:01.026 --> 00:06:03.162
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:05.197
Congratulations
on both the campaign

00:06:05.197 --> 00:06:07.200
and this really, really
wonderful book.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.934
-Oh, thank you.
-Thank you so much.

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:10.436
Marc Bernardin, everybody.

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:12.872
"Adora and the Distance"
is on sale now.

